1] Swapping Two Numbers Without a Third Variable [XOR]
Approach:

XOR has a property:

A ^ A = 0
A ^ 0 = A

Using these properties, we can swap two numbers without using any extra variable.

Take:

A = 5
B = 6

Binary:

5 = 101
6 = 110

Step 1

A = A ^ B;
101
110
---
011 = 3

A = 3
B = 6

Step 2

B = A ^ B;
011
110
---
101 = 5

A = 3
B = 5

Step 3

A = A ^ B;
011
101
---
110 = 6

A = 6
B = 5

Numbers are swapped.

Why does it work?

After the first XOR,

A = Original A ^ Original B

When we XOR it again with Original B, we get

(A ^ B) ^ B
= A ^ (B ^ B)
= A ^ 0
= A

Similarly,

(A ^ B) ^ A
= B

Hence both numbers get swapped.

Time Complexity: O(1)
Only three XOR operations are performed.

Space Complexity: O(1)
No extra variable is used.

Code:
int A = 5;
int B = 6;

A = A ^ B;
B = A ^ B;
A = A ^ B;
